Associate dimensions to all constants and variables as bookkeeping for how quantities appear in Newton's equations. The basic dimensions are length $L$, time $T$, and mass $M$; there can be others (such as charge), depending on the problem. For example,
$
  [dot(x)] = L T^(-1), quad [dot.double(x)] = L T^(-2), quad [F] = M L T^(-2), quad [E] = M L^2 T^(-2).
$

The fundamental principles are

- $["LHS"] = ["RHS"]$,

- all arguments of nontrivial functions (involving sums of different powers) must be dimensionless.

If the exponents in an ansatz like $T = f(theta_0) g^A l^B m^C$ are not all fixed, a dimensionless ratio remains.
